WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.649 --> 00:00:05.950
In this video, we'll start looking at real-time lighting in Unreal Engine

2
00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.039
and working with post-processing volumes.

3
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:12.740
An easy way we can start changing the rendering settings for our level

4
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.740
is by adding in a post-process volume.

5
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.539
This will allow us to create specific overrides

6
00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:22.839
for rendering settings when we are within the volume

7
00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:28.239
or we can also enable the checkbox for infinite extend

8
00:00:28.239 --> 00:00:31.839
to apply these settings to the entire level

9
00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:35.539
whether we're inside the volume or not.

10
00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:37.740
The two things we're going to look at here

11
00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.039
are the exposure settings and the bloom settings.

12
00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:47.640
In my own personal experience, I find that the auto exposure

13
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.240
that is set by default in Unreal Engine

14
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.240
can make it a little difficult when trying to balance

15
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.439
all of your different lights and get a specific look

16
00:00:56.439 --> 00:00:59.840
like evening or nighttime inside of your game.

17
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.939
And so by switching it to manual

18
00:01:01.939 --> 00:01:04.340
and changing our exposure compensation,

19
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.739
we can get more of an evening look.

20
00:01:06.739 --> 00:01:09.840
And then as well, we can affect the power of bloom

21
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.700
in order to make our stage pillars glow even brighter.

22
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.060
So let's take a look.

23
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.489
We can add a post-process volume quickly

24
00:01:23.489 --> 00:01:26.790
from the add actor button up here

25
00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:32.299
coming down to visual effects instead of volumes.

26
00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:37.060
Click and drag our post-process volume into the level.

27
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.359
So it doesn't look like anything's happened.

28
00:01:39.359 --> 00:01:42.260
Right now it still has all the same default configurations.

29
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.959
This is what we see around us.

30
00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:46.819
And we're also outside of the box.

31
00:01:46.819 --> 00:01:49.519
To give you an example of what this might look like,

32
00:01:49.519 --> 00:01:53.819
we'll change our exposure compensation here to 0.1

33
00:01:53.819 --> 00:01:57.920
when we are in our metering mode to manual.

34
00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.920
Actually, we can just leave this at 1.

35
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.980
And so when we go in the box, very dark.

36
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:05.980
Sun's up there.

37
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.180
Out of the box, very bright.

38
00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.680
So what if we want to see this, though,

39
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.680
throughout the entire level?

40
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.580
Like I mentioned a minute ago, we

41
00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:20.229
can search for infinite extend, have that enabled,

42
00:02:20.229 --> 00:02:24.129
and now the entire scene will be affected by these settings

43
00:02:24.129 --> 00:02:27.419
that we apply here in the volume.

44
00:02:27.419 --> 00:02:30.979
So I'm going to bring this up, though.

45
00:02:30.979 --> 00:02:35.469
10, maybe 8.

46
00:02:35.469 --> 00:02:40.669
And I also want to change our bloom,

47
00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:43.750
which you were looking at up here,

48
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.150
and change the intensity of the bloom to 0.8.

49
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.250
And we'll see that the glow from our pillars

50
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.080
has gotten a little bit brighter.

51
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.479
I think we can also change our lighting here, too,

52
00:02:55.479 --> 00:03:01.520
by working with the position of our sun.

53
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.520
And we can make our sun go a little bit down lower.

54
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.990
I think it's here we go.

55
00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:15.039
Get some cool evening sky going on here.

56
00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:16.199
Yeah, that'll be good.

57
00:03:16.199 --> 00:03:19.240
And we can also change the intensity of our sun

58
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.599
here as well.

59
00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:26.560
We want to see a little bit more of a afternoon light.

60
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.919
And I think maybe let's ramp up

61
00:03:28.919 --> 00:03:34.740
the bloom a little bit more for today.

62
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:35.939
Let's process volume.

63
00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:39.020
Let's put that to 1 or even 2.

64
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.069
We can see what that's doing a little more clearly.

65
00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:46.080
We're unchecked versus checked.

66
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.419
A little subtle for now, but that's OK.

67
00:03:50.419 --> 00:03:53.020
So now that we have our manual exposure set,

68
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.120
let's add in a few more lights

69
00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.620
and tweak their settings in order to get the look

70
00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.620
that we're going for inside of the scene.

71
00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.240
So to add light actors,

72
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.139
once again, we can come here to our Add Actor button,

73
00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.740
search for lights.

74
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.639
We already have a directional light in the scene

75
00:04:10.639 --> 00:04:13.340
and it's recommended to not have more than one.

76
00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:18.149
So instead, I'm going to add a spotlight

77
00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:21.250
and we can see its cone coming down.

78
00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.449
I'm going to add this spotlight

79
00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:27.449
I think kind of off to the front over here.

80
00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:34.480
We can angle it by pulling up our rotator gizmo.

81
00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.680
I'm going to change its intensity to 12.

82
00:04:37.779 --> 00:04:43.569
Let's try 20, 40, there we go.

83
00:04:43.569 --> 00:04:48.029
Let's rotate it a little bit more.

84
00:04:48.029 --> 00:04:53.439
I'm going to do an Alt drag off of it.

85
00:04:53.439 --> 00:04:56.439
Come over to the other side,

86
00:04:56.439 --> 00:05:01.160
be down a little more,

87
00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:12.319
rotate it around, there we go.

88
00:05:12.319 --> 00:05:15.120
It's looking a little bit more square on there.

89
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.439
We'll make this one a little cooler,

90
00:05:20.439 --> 00:05:25.560
give ourselves kind of a three-point lighting setup.

91
00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:30.339
We'll drag this one off over here,

92
00:05:30.339 --> 00:05:35.500
also bring it down over this way,

93
00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:44.720
bring it back around to face our dancer,

94
00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:57.300
up, over,

95
00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.800
and we can also,

96
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.300
let's maybe change our outer cone angle

97
00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:06.160
and make it a little bit brighter or narrower

98
00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:13.139
so we can really hone in on our character here.

99
00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:23.370
Okay, I think that's looking pretty good.

100
00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:27.000
Let's maybe bring up,

101
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.470
so obviously you can go all day fiddling around

102
00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.269
with these settings.

103
00:06:32.269 --> 00:06:39.160
We'll keep it a little more tight here,

104
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:50.959
that's zero, yeah, that's cool, awesome.

105
00:06:50.959 --> 00:06:53.060
In the next video, we'll take a look at some of the

106
00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.060
modeling tools in Unreal Engine

107
00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:58.360
and how we can use them to modify the meshes in our project.
